# Erdos Problem #99 - pentagonal two-contact tooth gate

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** AG42/Section-171 exceptional-face floor; exact force-hull residual conservation; Wegner residual quantization; empty-pentagon trapezoid endpoint; triangle-free contact-degree bound
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The empty-pentagon endpoint identifies every residual-zero isolate-free exceptional
pentagon with the flat unit trapezoid

\[
(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),
\left({3\over2},{\sqrt3\over2}\right),
\left({1\over2},{\sqrt3\over2}\right).
\]

Besides the almost-straight middle chain, this model has two more useful local
objects: at each end of the long base there is a two-edge boundary path of exact unit
contacts whose hidden chord is also unit in the limit. Thus a cheap actual pentagon
contains a **two-contact near-equilateral tooth**: two exact unit sides and only one
missing side.

Put

\[
X:=Q-f,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
f_*:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+0.00021
=0.167124643466509\ldots .
\tag{TT.1}
\]

For fixed `eta>0`, let `H_eta` be the maximum number of pairwise endpoint-disjoint
hidden chords `xz`, chosen from distinct pentagonal exceptional faces, for which there
is a common cyclic boundary neighbor `y` satisfying

\[
|x-y|=|y-z|=1,
\qquad
1<|x-z|<1+\eta.
\tag{TT.2}
\]

Let `T_eta` be the maximum number of pairwise vertex-disjoint triples `(x,y,z)` of the
same form. On every Type-II subsequence with

\[
R_F=o(D),
\tag{TT.3}
\]

we prove the weighted gates

\[
\boxed{
X+9H_\eta\ge(f_*-o(1))D,
}
\tag{TT.4}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
X+13T_\eta\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{TT.5}
\]

No assumption `X=o(D)` is needed for these two inequalities.

Consequently, if the high-degree excess is sublinear, then for every fixed `eta>0`,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {H_\eta\over D}\ge {f_*\over9}
=0.0185694048296121\ldots,
}
\tag{TT.6}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {T_\eta\over D}\ge {f_*\over13}
=0.0128557418051160\ldots .
}
\tag{TT.7}
\]

The first coefficient matches the best generic hidden-pair extraction, but its pairs
are now much more structured: both endpoints are boundary/core vertices and they have
a common source vertex joined to each by an exact unit contact. The second statement
makes the entire near-equilateral triples vertex-disjoint.

## 1. Good pentagons versus degree excess

Let `n_d` be the number of bounded exceptional faces of degree `d>=5`, and let `p_5`
be the pentagon count. The exact degree identity is

\[
X=Q-f=\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)n_d.
\tag{TT.8}
\]

If `N_{>=6}=sum_{d>=6}n_d`, then `N_{>=6}<=X`, so

\[
p_5=f-N_{\ge6}\ge f-X.
\tag{TT.9}
\]

The AG42/Section-171 face floor gives

\[
f\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
\tag{TT.10}
\]

Fix `eta>0`. By the empty-pentagon stability theorem, choose a geometric closeness
threshold small enough that an isolate-free pentagon in that neighborhood has both
leaf hidden diagonals shorter than `1+eta`. Let `tau_trap(eta)>0` be a corresponding
residual threshold, and put

\[
\theta(\eta):=\min\left\{
{1\over\sqrt3}-{1\over2},\tau_{trap}(\eta)
\right\}>0.
\tag{TT.11}
\]

If a pentagon has `r(P)<theta(eta)`, Wegner's first residual jump gives `z(P)=0`, and
trapezoid stability places it in the required neighborhood of the flat model.

All exceptional-face residuals are nonnegative and their sum is at most `R_F`. Hence,
on (TT.3), at most

\[
{R_F\over\theta(\eta)}=o(D)
\]

pentagons fail this fixed threshold. Let `G_eta` be the number of remaining good
pentagons. Combining this with (TT.9)--(TT.10) gives

\[
\boxed{
X+G_\eta\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{TT.12}
\]

This is the basic weighted pentagon count. It remains informative even when `X` is
linear.

## 2. Every good pentagon contains a two-contact near-equilateral tooth

Use the cyclic labels from the flat-trapezoid theorem:

\[
l,m,r,a,b,
\]

where `m` is the almost-flat middle point of the long base. In the equality model the
two leaf triples are

\[
(m,l,b),
\qquad
(m,r,a).
\tag{TT.13}
\]

Each triple uses two consecutive pentagon boundary edges. Therefore in every actual
good pentagon those two sides remain exact unit contacts. Trapezoid stability and the
choice of `theta(eta)` give

\[
|m-b|<1+\eta,
\qquad
|m-a|<1+\eta.
\tag{TT.14}
\]

Both hidden diagonals are strictly longer than one. Indeed, if a nonconsecutive pair
of boundary vertices of an actual exceptional contact face were at unit distance, its
straight unit-contact chord would lie inside the convex face and split it, contrary to
that pentagon being a face of the plane contact graph. Equivalently, either equality
in (TT.14) at one would create the corresponding unit equilateral contact triangle.
Thus both leaf triples satisfy (TT.2).

Choose one of the two leaf teeth from every good pentagon. The second choice is not
needed for the counting argument below.

## 3. Endpoint-disjoint hidden chords: loss only 1/9

From each chosen tooth retain only its hidden chord `xz`. Build a conflict graph on
these chords, joining two when they share an endpoint.

A source vertex in a triangle-free unit-contact graph has contact degree at most five.
Consequently it belongs to at most five distinct bounded face sectors, hence to at most
five selected pentagons. For one chosen chord `{x,z}`, at most four other chosen
chords can use `x` and at most four can use `z`. Therefore the chord-conflict graph has
maximum degree at most

\[
\boxed{8.}
\tag{TT.15}
\]

A greedy independent set keeps at least `G_eta/9` chords, so

\[
9H_\eta\ge G_\eta.
\tag{TT.16}
\]

Combining with (TT.12) proves (TT.4).

The unweighted consequence is

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{{X\over D},{H_\eta\over D}\right\}
\ge {f_*\over10}-o(1)
=0.0167124643466509\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{TT.17}
\]

## 4. Fully vertex-disjoint teeth: loss only 1/13

Now retain all three vertices of each chosen tooth. Two selected teeth conflict when
they share any source vertex. As above, a fixed source vertex belongs to at most five
selected pentagons, and there is only one chosen tooth per pentagon. Each tooth has
three vertices, so it conflicts with at most

\[
3(5-1)=12
\tag{TT.18}
\]

other selected teeth. A greedy independent set therefore has size at least
`G_eta/13`. Hence

\[
13T_\eta\ge G_\eta,
\tag{TT.19}
\]

which together with (TT.12) proves (TT.5).

The corresponding unweighted alternative is

\[
\boxed{
\max\left\{{X\over D},{T_\eta\over D}\right\}
\ge {f_*\over14}-o(1)
=0.0119374745333220\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{TT.20}
\]

## 5. Vanishing-gap diagonal

The fixed-`eta` arguments above can be diagonalized without changing the first-order
coefficients. Take `eta_j=1/j`. For each fixed `j`, the number of pentagons with
residual at least `theta(eta_j)` is `o(D)`. Choose an increasing sequence of diameter
thresholds so slowly that, on the interval assigned to `j`, this bad-face loss is
still `o(D)`. This defines a scale

\[
\eta_D\to0
\]

for which

\[
X+9H_{\eta_D}\ge(f_*-o(1))D,
\qquad
X+13T_{\eta_D}\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
\tag{TT.21}
\]

In particular, if `X=o(D)`, there are at least

\[
\left({f_*\over13}-o(1)\right)D
\]

pairwise vertex-disjoint actual source triples with

\[
|x-y|=|y-z|=1,
\qquad
1<|x-z|\le1+o(1).
\tag{TT.22}
\]

Thus the hard small-residual, low-degree-excess branch contains a macroscopic family
of almost unit equilateral triangles in which **two** sides are already exact active
contacts.

## 6. Relation to the other Groemer extractions

This result is not a numerical replacement for the generic Groemer matching; it is a
structural refinement of the pentagonal endpoint.

- The generic hidden-pair theorem gives the same limiting `f_*/9` pair coefficient but
  allows the pair to involve assigned isolates and supplies no common exact-contact
  neighbor.
- The generic anchored-triple theorem gives one exact unit side and two near-unit
  sides. Here every extracted triple has two exact unit sides and only one near-unit
  side.
- The whole-pentagon trapezoid theorem retains all five vertices and therefore loses a
  factor `1/21`. The present full-triple extraction needs only three vertices and loses
  `1/13`.
- The almost-flat-chain extraction uses the complementary local feature of the same
  pentagon: its near-straight middle contact chain. The two statements should be kept
  separate because their prospective deformation mechanisms are different.

## 7. Scope and remaining gate

- The theorem is conditional on `R_F=o(D)`.
- The weighted forms (TT.4)--(TT.5) do not assume `Q-f=o(D)`.
- Every tooth vertex is an actual source vertex on the boundary of a positive-contact
  exceptional face; no assigned interior isolate appears in a good pentagon.
- The hidden chord remains inactive and strictly longer than one in the actual source.
- No KKT multiplier is assigned to the hidden chord.
- No exact unit triangle or same-cardinality strict sub-`D` source is produced.

The remaining source-level question is sharper than generic near-contact activation:
can a globally diameter-minimizing triangle-free source contain linearly many
vertex-disjoint triples with two positive unit contacts at angle `pi/3+o(1)` while the
third side stays strictly above one? A successful finite-motion or equilibrium theorem
for that three-vertex object would close this pentagonal branch.

C-II and Level I remain open.
